Section 1: The Endocrine Signal

1.1 Pulse as Information

The endocrine system does not transmit via static concentration. Lightman & Conway-Campbell (2010) established that the HPA axis operates through discrete episodic bursts, and Veldhuis's deconvolution studies demonstrated that pulse frequency, amplitude, duration, and inter-pulse interval carry information independently of mean level. The system is a digital-analog hybrid transmitter, not a slowly varying chemical bath.

The critical result for this framework is Stavreva et al. (2009, Nature Cell Biology): pulsatile versus sustained glucocorticoid exposure produces measurably different chromatin remodeling at the same locus, at equal mean concentration. The pulse structure is the signal. Concentration is the carrier. The distinction is not cosmetic — it is the difference between two entirely different patterns of gene regulation at the same average hormone level.

This fixes the input object for the entire pipeline: a one-dimensional time series x(t) of hormone concentration, sampled at the ultradian step τ ≈ 90 min. Everything downstream — the phase-space reconstruction, the leaky-kernel integration, the chromatin writes — takes this time series as its source.

1.2 The Three Brains as Decoders

Part II §1.2.2 mapped the three brains functionally onto the P-V-C triad. The cardiac brain drives the tissue-scale dielectric substrate (P); the enteric brain anchors the body-to-Earth reference frame (V); the cephalic brain operates as the adaptive matcher (C). All three decode the same systemic endocrine signal through tissue-specific chromatin accessibility.

The mapping is functional, not anatomical. The cardiac brain is not "in" the proprioceptive system; rather, its electromagnetic and mechanical oscillations directly shape the myofascial lattice's piezoelectric response, which is what the P-interface reads. The enteric brain's metabolic and neurochemical output anchors the body-to-Earth reference frame that the V-interface operates against. The cephalic brain's predictive dynamics run on the integrated P+V substrate.

The three brains are not three separate genomes. They carry the same 24 chromosome types. What differs is chromatin weighting — the tissue-specific topological accessibility of that genome in each neural system, established in Roadmap Epigenomics and ENCODE data. The same pulse train drives distinct responses in liver, heart, and brain because the local chromatin state filters the incoming signal differently. The three brains are three decoders of the same systemic endocrine output.

1.3 The Five Endocrine Axes as the 5-Fold Generator

Part II §1.2.1 introduced the endocrine dual (testosterone/estrogen) as the biological instantiation of the 5-fold pentagram inflation. The five endocrine axes — HPA, HPG, HPT, somatotropic, lactotropic — are the five channels through which that dual acts.

The tier structure (hypothalamus → pituitary → target gland) is the 3-fold root. The axis structure is the 5-fold inflation. The two are coprime: gcd(3,5) = 1. This is why the endocrine system has both a universal three-tier architecture and five distinct axes without one reducing to the other. If the tier count shared a factor with the axis count, the endocrine system would collapse into a degenerate architecture; the coprimality is what allows both to be simultaneously present.

1.4 The Ultradian Pulse Train

The 12-hour high-vigilance phase of the diurnal cycle contains approximately 8 major ultradian cortisol peaks at the classic ~90-min inter-pulse interval:

\frac{12 \text{ h}}{90 \text{ min}} = 8.

This is a model choice — ultradian cortisol pulses continue through sleep — but it is clean, and it matches the inherited channel count from Theory/Residual_integer_8.md. The 8-dot chain is the temporal template onto which habits, routines, and sustained endocrine exposures imprint.

The habit-to-baseline chain is worth stating explicitly because it is the core of what this Part argues:

Behavior repeats → period locks to the endocrine pulse train → the axis engaged by the behavior accumulates a stronger pulse → the pulse pattern stabilizes → chromatin writes the stabilized pattern → the baseline persists.

Once locked and weighted, the pattern is what the chromatin system writes. The written state is what persists beyond the behavior itself.

