Section 3: Phase-Space and Leaky Kernel

3.1 Phase-Space Reconstruction

The endocrine signal is a time series. Following Takens (1981), the delay-coordinate vector

\mathbf{X}(t) = \big(x(t),\, x(t+\tau),\, \dots,\, x(t+(d-1)\tau)\big)

reconstructs a manifold M ⊂ ℝ^d that is topologically equivalent to the underlying dynamical system, provided d > 2n for n active degrees of freedom. The reconstructed manifold is the emergent geometry of the endocrine state space — not an imposed shape, but one derived from the signal itself.

This is the correct role of geometry in the framework. It is not a geometric overlay applied to biology from an unrelated domain; it is the emergent phase-space structure that the endocrine signal traces out. The Jacobian classification in §3.2 is meaningful because the manifold is real.

3.2 Stability Classification

The local dynamics at equilibrium X* are governed by the Jacobian

\mathbf{J} = \left.\frac{\partial \mathbf{F}}{\partial \mathbf{X}}\right|_{\mathbf{X} = \mathbf{X}^*},

whose eigenvalues λ_j = α_j + iβ_j classify the regime:

Regime Eigenvalue condition Physiological interpretation
Stable node α_j < 0, β_j = 0 Aperiodic recovery, low allostatic load
Stable focus α_j < 0, some β_j ≠ 0 Healthy ~90-min ultradian pulse train
Limit cycle isolated periodic orbit Diurnal entrainment
Unstable focus / Hopf pair crosses Re = 0 Loss of pulsatility, transition to saturation
Chaotic attractor positive Lyapunov exponent Chronic desynchronization

This is standard linear stability theory applied to the leaky-kernel ODE. It classifies the dynamical regime — how the signal arrives. It does not classify the chromatin channels — where the signal is written. The two layers are separate and should not be conflated. The dynamical regime tells us whether the pulse train is coherent; the channel count tells us how that coherent pulse train is addressed onto chromatin.

3.3 The Leaky Kernel

The chromatin state integrates the endocrine signal rather than tracking it instantaneously:

\frac{dX}{dt} = -\lambda X + S(t),

where S(t) is the pulse input and λ is the recovery rate.

Real recovery is multi-timescale: fast cortisol clearance (minutes), slow HPA axis reset (hours), very slow structural remodeling (weeks–months). The honest form is a spectrum:

X(t) = \sum_k A_k e^{-\lambda_k t} + X_{\text{baseline}},

with λ_k spanning several orders of magnitude. The constants are measurable from cortisol recovery curves after acute stress, and their divergence across timescales is what produces the difference between a system that resets quickly and one that accumulates load.

3.4 Allostatic Load

Allostatic load is the integrated un-cleared residue:

\Lambda_{\text{allo}} = \int_0^T \left|X(t) - X_{\text{baseline}}\right| dt.

This connects to McEwen's quantitative allostatic-load framework (1998). The inflammatory viscosity load η_inflam in Part II's Re_ε formula is the same quantity at the network scale.

The endocrine-motif geometry — the nested pulsatility hierarchy of ultradian, circadian, and infradian scales — is what carries across time. The signal that reaches chromatin is not the hormone concentration; it is the pulse geometry. Lightman's group showed this directly: pulsatile cortisol drives NR3C1 transcription differently than constant cortisol at equal mean. The ultradian rhythm is the signal; concentration is the carrier; geometry is the content.

This is why the correlation between pulse geometry and chromatin state is stable across studies while the content of the sustained condition is not. What was the person experiencing during the exposure is irrelevant to the chromatin mark. The geometry is what propagates.

