Section 4: Hypergraph Coordination

4.1 From Dyadic to Hypergraph Payoffs

Part II §3.5 modeled strategic interaction as a two-organism encounter with dielectric viscosity reduction. Real coordination is N-organism. The payoff function generalizes from a pairwise matrix to a field density over a hyperedge:

\Pi(v_i) = \int_{\Omega_e} \left(\Lambda_{CG}(\mathbf{x}) \cdot \nabla \mathcal{J}_{K^+} - \eta_{\text{inflam}}(\mathbf{x})\right) d\mathbf{x},

where Λ_CG is the Cognitive Glue Index (Part II §3.5.2; Def1 of CLAIMS.md), 𝒥_K⁺ is the potassium wave velocity through the shared substrate, and η_inflam is the inflammatory viscosity load. The strategic yield for node v_i is determined by the total structural integration of the hyperedge it occupies, not by pairwise transactions.

The reformulation is not cosmetic. Hypergraph public goods games with heterogeneous investment produce qualitatively different cooperation dynamics than their dyadic counterparts, including the stabilization of cooperation at lower investment thresholds. The field-density formulation captures this.

4.2 Order-Dependent Composition

Inside a phase-locked hyperedge, the order in which nodes commit restricts the state space available to every subsequent node. Earlier commitments shrink the reachable configuration space. The composition is order-dependent:

g_{\text{group}} = \prod_{i=1}^{n} \sigma_{GJ}^{(i)} \cdot \rho_{OXTR}^{(i)},

where the product preserves order.

This is a non-commutative monoid, not a group — there are no inverses. The order-dependence is the substantive claim. Calling it non-Abelian group structure overstates the algebra and invites dismissal from readers with algebra training. Order-dependent composition is enough, and it is empirically true: receiving hormone A before hormone B yields a different physiological outcome than receiving B before A, and group commitment sequences produce correspondingly different final states.

4.3 De-Symmetrization and the Ridge

As the group coordinates on The Ridge, the system undergoes spontaneous symmetry breaking: the uncoordinated possibilities of independent agents collapse into a discrete subgroup H ⊂ G. This is the mathematical signature of collective decision-making, not a failure mode.

If the system over-corrects, losing all fluid flexibility, it enters the hyper-rigid Valley state where H ossifies and blocks subsequent adaptation. The Ridge is not a fixed point but a bounded interval of Re_ε in which H retains residual symmetry sufficient for reconfiguration.

4.4 The Five Strategic Regimes

The payoff integral generates five regimes under recursive scale-inflation:

· The Abyss — Re_ε → 0, low Λ_CG, dominant defection, pattern memory collapse
· The Turbulent Grid — unstable oscillation, no stable subgroup forms
· The Ridge — 3.0 ≤ Re_ε ≤ 15.0, resonant cooperation, ℛ-operator active
· The Valley — Re_ε > 15.0, hyper-rigid ossification, frozen conformity
· The Cross-Kingdom Register — payoff integration extends past species boundary

The first four were established in Part II §5.5. The fifth extends the hyperedge to include non-human biological actors. This is treated in the biology-suite Technical Note on cross-kingdom waveguides and is not developed further here.

4.5 The Ridge–Valley Bifurcation

The transition from Ridge (resonant cooperation) to Valley (rigid ossification) is not a gradual drift. It is a bifurcation in the strategic landscape. The control parameter is the ratio of demethylation cost to available ATP: when ΔG_reconfig / ATP_budget exceeds a threshold, the system cannot afford belief revision, and the strategic posture locks.

This is the physical meaning of the "strategic rigidity" identification from Part II §0.6. An organism with deep methylation at NR3C1 cannot simply "decide" to trust a partner. The metabolic cost of rewriting the chromatin state exceeds the ATP available during the interaction window. Defection is not chosen; it is the only strategy encoded in the affordable chromatin state.

4.6 The EEG Hyperscanning Gate

The scalar phase-lock criterion in Part II §3.5 is necessary but not sufficient. Two organisms with high scalar alignment but zero true coupling can still pass the scalar test, because volume conduction produces apparent coherence. The EEG hyperscanning layer (separate module, §9) provides the field-level gate: it reconstructs source-space activity, computes single-trial imaginary coherence (which suppresses zero-lag leakage), and only flags phase-lock when source-level coupling exceeds threshold.

The gate is optional. When disabled, the scalar criterion governs. When enabled, phase-lock requires both scalar alignment and field-level iCOH above threshold.

