Section 7: The Institutional Layer

7.1 What This Section Introduces

§4 describes coordination among individuals. The institutional layer describes coordination among organizations. The mechanism is structurally identical — shared substrate, coupling density, threshold — but the actors are institutions rather than organisms.

7.2 The Swarm Mechanism

When an anomalous framework appears, dominant institutions converge on a defensive posture without central directive. This is the equilibrium of locally rational responses by actors sharing a resource environment. DiMaggio & Powell (1983) identified the three mechanisms: coercive, mimetic, and normative isomorphism.

The specific activity is what Gieryn (1983) called boundary work: the practices by which institutions demarcate legitimate from illegitimate knowledge. Boundary work is a constitutive feature of any professionalized knowledge domain, not a corruption of it.

7.3 Operational Closure

Luhmann's apparatus applies. Social systems are operationally closed: each reproduces itself through its own operations, using a binary code meaningless to other systems. The academic system's code is true/false. An anomaly with no bearing on that distinction cannot be registered — not from bias, but from the absence of the operation required to process it.

7.4 The Enclosure Ratio

The enclosure ratio 𝒦 measures the proportion of a system's operations that are internally referential. As 𝒦 → 1, the system becomes fully self-referential; as 𝒦 → 0, it loses internal coherence. The productive range is narrow: enough closure to hold a position, enough openness to register a genuine anomaly.

7.5 Companion Note

The full development of the institutional layer, with its own citations (Bourdieu, Collins, Kuhn, Luhmann), is in docs/swarm_apparatus.md (forthcoming). This section serves as the internal anchor.

