Section 10: Falsification Protocols

Protocols continue Part II's numbering (51–78). This Part adds 79–84.

Protocol 79: Phase-Space Reconstruction of the HPA Axis

Objective: Confirm that pulsatile cortisol time series reconstruct a low-dimensional manifold with stability regime consistent with the leaky-kernel prediction.

Methodology: Continuous cortisol sampling at 10-min intervals over 72 h in healthy adults under controlled sleep. Delay-coordinate embedding with τ set to the ultradian step. Jacobian estimated from local linear fits.

Metric Verification: Falsified if the reconstructed manifold does not yield a stable-focus regime during healthy ultradian cycling. Validation requires stable-focus classification in >80% of healthy subjects, with transition to unstable-focus under acute stress challenge.

Protocol 80: Chromatin Writing Quantization

Objective: Test whether chromatin response to pulsatile endocrine input is quantized at the 8-channel level.

Methodology: In vitro glucocorticoid pulse stimulation of hepatocyte and neuron cultures at controlled pulse frequencies. Single-cell ATAC-seq at fixed time points. Dimensionality reduction to test for 8-cluster structure.

Metric Verification: Falsified if the chromatin response manifold has effective dimension significantly different from 8 across both cell types. Validation requires consistent 8-fold quantization and correspondence of the clusters to the residual channel labels.

Protocol 81: Common-Prior Methylation Alignment

Objective: Test whether groups that coordinate on The Ridge share NR3C1/FKBP5/OXTR methylation topology more than groups that do not.

Methodology: Longitudinal cohort with methylation assays at baseline and after a controlled coordination task. Comparison of within-group variance before and after.

Metric Verification: Falsified if within-group variance does not decrease significantly after successful coordination. Validation requires measurable alignment with effect size scaling with group Re_ε.

Protocol 82: Ridge–Valley Bifurcation Mapping

Objective: Confirm that the Ridge–Valley transition is a bifurcation at ΔG_reconfig / ATP_budget ≈ 1 rather than a graded drift.

Methodology: Manipulate available ATP (via metabolic challenge) and demethylation cost (via DNMT inhibitor dose) in vitro in a cell line with an inducible strategic marker. Measure chromatin rewrite rate as a function of the ratio.

Metric Verification: Falsified if rewrite rate scales continuously with the ratio. Validation requires a sharp onset at ratio ≈ 1 with measurable hysteresis.

Protocol 83: Hypergraph Order-Dependence

Objective: Test whether group coordination outcome depends on commitment order in hyperedges.

Methodology: N-agent coordination games with controlled commitment sequencing (ascending vs. descending OXTR activation order). Compare final group state and payoff distribution.

Metric Verification: Falsified if ascending and descending assemblies converge to the same final state within measurement error. Validation requires measurable divergence scaling with OXTR gradient.

Protocol 84: Intergenerational Propagation

Objective: Determine whether offspring chromatin state tracks parental sustained-condition patterns, or whether it is re-set by the offspring's own developmental environment.

Methodology: Cross-fostering design. Parents under known sustained conditions (measured via NR3C1/FKBP5 methylation at study entry) are paired with non-exposed foster parents. Offspring methylation is assayed at matched developmental stages.

Metric Verification: If offspring methylation patterns track foster-parent rather than biological-parent patterns, propagation is through the postnatal developmental environment. If offspring track biological-parent patterns despite cross-fostering, propagation is through germline or intrauterine mechanisms. Both outcomes are informative; the design separates them.

